<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Maximilian of Baden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Max von Baden, Prince Max of Baden · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> was a German liberal aristocrat who served as the last chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in October and November 1918. He sought an armistice with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and on 9 November 1918 announced the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> on his own authority before handing power to the Social Democrat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a>. In 1920 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schule-schloss-salem">Schule Schloss Salem</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-hahn">Kurt Hahn</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden.jpg" alt="Prince Maximilian of Baden" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author Originally uploaded to Wikimedia Commons by BArchBot Cropped and reuploaded to Wikimedia Commons by Emiya1980 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R04103,_Prinz_Max_von_Baden(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maximilian was born in Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> on 10 July 1867, the son of Prince Wilhelm Max of Baden and Princess Maria Maximilianovna of Leuchtenberg, a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugene-de-beauharnais">Eugène de Beauharnais</a>. He studied law and cameralism at Leipzig University and trained as an officer in the Prussian Army. After the death of his uncle Grand Duke Frederick I of Baden in 1907 he became heir to the grand-ducal throne of his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-grand-duke-of-baden">Frederick II</a>, whose marriage remained childless, and he became president of the upper house of Baden's parliament.</p><p>He married Princess Marie Louise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> in 1900. In 1914 he became honorary president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> section of the German Red Cross, working for prisoners of war, and he spoke against the resumption of unrestricted submarine warfare in 1917.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the Supreme Army Command told the government in late September 1918 that the front was about to collapse, Chancellor Georg von Hertling's cabinet resigned and Max was appointed Imperial Chancellor and minister-president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> on 3 October 1918. He had reservations about the plan and said openly that he was no politician, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> convinced him to take the post. His government rested on the majority parties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>, and for the first time included Social Democrats, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philipp-scheidemann">Philipp Scheidemann</a> and Gustav Bauer. He sought an armistice on the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a>'s Fourteen Points, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff">Erich Ludendorff</a> turned to continued fighting, Max had him dismissed.</p><p>A political amnesty was passed, and in late October the constitution was amended so that the chancellor answered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>. As revolution spread in early November, Max urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> to abdicate. At noon on 9 November 1918 he announced the abdication of the emperor and the renunciation of the crown prince without waiting for their consent. He then resigned and handed the chancellorship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a> in an unconstitutional move, and refused Ebert's request to stay on as regent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Max returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and spent the rest of his life in retirement. He rejected a mandate to the Weimar National Assembly of 1919 and in 1920 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schule-schloss-salem">Schule Schloss Salem</a> boarding school with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-hahn">Kurt Hahn</a>. He published Völkerbund und Rechtsfriede, Die moralische Offensive and Erinnerungen und Dokumente. In 1928 he became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a> as Margrave of Baden, and he died at Salem on 6 November 1929. Although regarded as a weak chancellor, he is seen as having done much to bring about the transition to a government based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> majority, which made Ebert's government acceptable to some conservative forces in the bureaucracy and military.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prince Maximilian of Baden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The German revolution, begun by the sailors' mutiny at Kiel, reached Berlin on 9 November 1918, when <b>Max von Baden</b> announced the abdication of Wilhelm II as Emperor and King of Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeat in the First World War brought revolution: on 9 November 1918 Chancellor <b>Max von Baden</b> announced the abdication of William II, who fled to the Netherlands and formally renounced the thrones of Prussia and Germany on 28 November.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 9 November 1918 Philipp Scheidemann of the Majority Social Democrats proclaimed the German Republic at the Reichstag building, the abdication of Kaiser Wilhelm II was announced, and <b>Prince Max of Baden</b> handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>With revolution spreading, Chancellor <b>Max von Baden</b> announced the Kaiser's abdication and Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic in Berlin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-9">9 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1920 Kurt Hahn and <b>Prince Max of Baden</b> founded the Schule Schloss Salem boarding school there.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-salem">Imperial Abbey of Salem</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Baden-Baden, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-baden-wurttemberg">Salem</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.76,8.24&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Baden-Baden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Prince Maximilian of Baden"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5463 5926h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5463 5926h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.76,8.24&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
